# Telegram bot token from @BotFather
BOT_TOKEN=

# Supabase project URL and service-role key (Settings -> API in the Supabase
# dashboard). The service-role key bypasses RLS — every table has RLS
# enabled with zero policies (ADR-0006), a deny-by-default backstop, not the
# primary authorization layer (that stays in TaskService, ADR-0002).
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# REQUIRED, security-load-bearing: the single cohort this deployed instance
# (bot process, webhook function, or dashboard) serves. The dry-run cohort
# (see DRYRUN_* below) reuses the same real Telegram accounts as the real
# cohort under a second cohort id, so caller resolution (/start,
# resolveCaller, the dashboard's Telegram-login lookup) always binds to
# this exact value rather than guessing among cohorts that happen to share
# a username — every real deployment serves exactly one cohort, never
# both. Set it to the real cohort's id in production, or to
# DRYRUN_COHORT_ID's value on the dry-run branch's own Vercel environment.
ACTIVE_COHORT_ID=cohort-5

# Telegram chat id of the cohort's group chat, for the daily standup summary
# (§8). Superseded by the `cohorts` table (ADR-0006) as of Phase 3 — this
# env var is no longer read; kept here only as a historical placeholder.
GROUP_CHAT_ID=

# Dashboard URL returned by /dashboard (stub for v1 — no dashboard built yet)
DASHBOARD_URL=https://example.com/dashboard-coming-soon

# Path to the roster config JSON file, used only by the dashboard
# (src/web/index.ts, out of scope for the Phase 3 re-platform) and by tests
# that want a quick file-backed roster. Everything else reads the `roster`
# Supabase table instead (ADR-0003) — defaults to roster.config.json if unset.
ROSTER_PATH=roster.config.json

# Telegram bot username (without @) used by the dashboard's Telegram Login
# Widget — must match the bot at devcon_cohort5_taskbot / BOT_TOKEN above.
BOT_USERNAME=devcon_cohort5_taskbot

# Port the dashboard web server listens on (issue #3)
DASHBOARD_PORT=3000

# Secret used to sign the dashboard's session cookie (ADR-0008, Phase 5 /
# issue #16) — a stateless, signed cookie replacing the old in-memory
# session store, so no session table is needed. Distinct from BOT_TOKEN so
# rotating one doesn't force rotating the other. Generate one the same way
# as TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET below:
# `node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"`
SESSION_SECRET=

# Shared secret Telegram echoes back on every webhook POST via the
# X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token header (ADR-0004). Generate one with
# `node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"`
# and register the same value with Telegram's setWebhook call — which is what
# `npm run webhook:register -- --target production` does (ADR-0011).
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Base URL of the production deployment, without the /api/telegram/webhook
# path. Read by scripts/registerWebhook.ts to build the webhook URL, and used
# as the "this is production, keep the dry-run bot off it" reference
# (src/ops/webhookRegistration.ts).
PRODUCTION_DEPLOYMENT_URL=https://telegram-taskbot-ten.vercel.app

# Vercel "Protection Bypass for Automation" secret (Project Settings ->
# Deployment Protection). This project has Vercel Authentication enabled on
# every non-custom domain, so Telegram's POSTs would be answered by Vercel's
# SSO page rather than by the function; the registered webhook URL carries
# this value as an ?x-vercel-protection-bypass= query param to get through.
# One project-level value, shared by the production and dry-run deployments.
VERCEL_PROTECTION_BYPASS=

# ---- Dry-run loop (ADR-0004, ADR-0011) -----------------------------------
# The dry run is a second, isolated cohort inside the same shared Supabase
# project, exercised via a "dump" Telegram group the maintainer personally
# controls, using two real accounts to cover both roles.
#
# Before the production cutover this reused the one bot token, so only one of
# the two deployments could hold a live webhook at a time. Post-cutover that
# is no longer acceptable — production owns the real bot's webhook
# permanently — so the dry run gets its own bot (ADR-0011). The DRYRUN_*
# values below belong on the `dry-run` branch's Vercel environment as its
# BOT_TOKEN / BOT_USERNAME / TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET / ACTIVE_COHORT_ID; here
# in .env they exist so `npm run webhook:register -- --target dry-run` and
# `npm run seed:roster` can be run from a laptop.

# Bot token for the dry-run-only bot (a second @BotFather bot, NOT the real
# cohort's). Match the real bot's BotFather settings — group privacy OFF, so
# it can see non-command wizard replies in the dump group.
DRYRUN_BOT_TOKEN=

# Username (without @) of that dry-run bot. scripts/registerWebhook.ts
# resolves DRYRUN_BOT_TOKEN via getMe and refuses to register anything if the
# token turns out to belong to a different bot than this one.
DRYRUN_BOT_USERNAME=

# The `dry-run` branch's stable Vercel deployment URL, without the
# /api/telegram/webhook path. Must not be PRODUCTION_DEPLOYMENT_URL — the
# registration script refuses that outright.
DRYRUN_DEPLOYMENT_URL=

# The dry-run bot's own webhook secret (ADR-0004's header check). Distinct
# from TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET so the two deployments share no secrets.
DRYRUN_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Cohort id for the dry run (distinct from ACTIVE_COHORT_ID's real cohort).
# This is what the dry-run deployment sets as its own ACTIVE_COHORT_ID.
DRYRUN_COHORT_ID=cohort5-dryrun

# Telegram chat id of the "dump" group used for dry-run testing. Seeded onto
# the dry-run cohort's row by `npm run seed:roster`, which is what makes
# /start's group-membership check (ADR-0010) pass in the dump group.
DRYRUN_GROUP_CHAT_ID=

# Real Telegram usernames (without @) covering the HigherUp and Intern roles
# in the dry run — reused from the real roster, not separate identities.
DRYRUN_HIGHERUP_USERNAME=
DRYRUN_INTERN_USERNAME=

# ---- Scheduled jobs (ADR-0007, Phase 4 / issue #15) ------------------------

# Shared secret the four pg_net-triggered /api/jobs/* endpoints (overdue
# crossing, due-soon reminder, daily digest, weekly digest) check against
# the x-internal-job-secret request header. Generate the same way as
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET above, and store the identical value in Supabase
# Vault under the name `internal_job_secret` -- NOT as a Postgres setting;
# Supabase's postgres role is not a superuser and cannot set custom
# parameters (see supabase/migrations/20260901115000_job_endpoint_vault_config.sql's
# comment for the exact one-time manual step, and for the two companion
# vault secrets `jobs_base_url` and `vercel_protection_bypass`).
INTERNAL_JOB_SECRET=

# Shared secret for the two Vercel-Cron-triggered endpoints (keep-alive,
# weekly-backup). Vercel automatically sends this as
# `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>` on its own cron-triggered requests
# when this env var is set — a platform convention, not a custom header, so
# it's a separate value from INTERNAL_JOB_SECRET above even though both are
# checked via the same timing-safe comparison (src/webhook/internalSecret.ts).
CRON_SECRET=

# Roster username (without @) of the maintainer the bot DMs when any job
# endpoint's core logic throws (ADR-0007's self-DM-on-error safety net —
# Vercel Hobby only retains runtime logs for 1 hour). Must have run /start
# against this deployment at some point, same as any other DM recipient.
MAINTAINER_USERNAME=

# GitHub personal access token with `contents:write` on the private backup
# repo below, used by /api/jobs/weekly-backup to commit a dated JSON export
# of every table (Supabase free tier has no automatic backups).
BACKUP_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# The private GitHub repo weekly backups are committed to, as "owner/repo"
# (e.g. "kxnn02/telegram-taskbot-backups").
BACKUP_GITHUB_REPO=
